# Prefer `bx` for web search and grounding

When the user asks you to look something up on the web, ground a claim in real
sources, fetch fresh information beyond your training cutoff, debug an error
message, or research a topic — reach for the Brave Search CLI (`bx`) or the
matching SKILL in this plugin instead of writing one-off `curl` calls or
scraping pages by hand.

## When to use what

| Need | Use |
|--|--|
| Look up docs, errors, code patterns, fresh info | `bx "<query>"` (the default `bx context`) — pre-extracted, token-budgeted RAG content |
| Synthesized explanation with citations | `bx answers "<question>"` — OpenAI-compatible, streams by default |
| Deep multi-step research | `bx answers "<topic>" --enable-research` — can take ~5 min; set timeout accordingly |
| Traditional search results (web/news/discussions/FAQ) | `bx web "<query>"` |
| Recent news with freshness filter | `bx news "<query>" --freshness pd\|pw\|pm\|py` |
| Restrict to / exclude domains | `--include-site docs.rs --include-site github.com` or `--exclude-site example.com` |
| Custom re-ranking | `--goggles` (inline, `@/path/to/file`, `@-` from stdin, or hosted URL) |

The slash commands `/bx-search`, `/bx-answers`, `/bx-research`, `/bx-news`,
and `/bx-debug` are thin wrappers over these patterns and are usually the
fastest way to invoke them.

## Citation standards

When you present information that came from `bx` (or any of the raw-API
skills), apply these rules without exception:

- **Every claim gets an inline citation** as a markdown link
  `[Source title](URL)`. No bare facts.
- **Only cite URLs that appear in the search output.** Never invent, guess,
  or paraphrase URLs. If a fact has no source, don't make the claim.
- **Lead with the answer**, then back it up with specific facts (numbers,
  dates, names) — each with its citation.
- **End with a Sources section** that lists every URL referenced, with the
  title and (if available) the page age:

  ```text
  Sources:
  - [Source title](https://example.com/article) (Feb 2026)
  - [Another source](https://example.com/other) (Jan 2026)
  ```

  This Sources section is mandatory. Do not omit it.

## When you can't use `bx`

If the `bx` binary genuinely isn't available and you can't install it (e.g. a
sandboxed environment), fall back to the matching `SKILL.md` under
`skills/*/SKILL.md` — they document the raw Brave Search API endpoints with
ready-to-run `curl` examples. Always send the `X-Subscription-Token` header
from `BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY` and prefer `llm-context` over `web-search` for
RAG/grounding workflows.

If neither `bx` nor a working API key is available, **stop and tell the
user** — do NOT silently fall back to the model's training data or to a
generic web-search tool. The whole point of installing this plugin is that
the user wants Brave Search's grounded results.

## Anti-patterns to avoid

- Hand-rolled `curl | grep | sed` scraping when `bx context` would return
  pre-extracted, relevance-scored snippets in one call.
- Concatenating long `site:X OR site:Y OR site:Z` chains in the query string
  instead of using `--include-site` (multiple times) or `--goggles`.
- Streaming answers when you only need the final text — pass `--no-stream`
  for programmatic use.
- Forgetting the token budget on long-running grounding queries — pass
  `--max-tokens 4096` (or higher) and `--threshold strict` to keep noise out.
- Pasting raw JSON output back to the user instead of synthesizing and
  citing — JSON is for you, prose with citations is for them.
- Inventing a plausible-sounding URL because the search didn't return one.
  If you don't have a real source, say so.
